      "writtenOn": "2026-03-12T22:14:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "One of the conditions for `block_modifications()` to succeed is that there are no mappings at the time it is invoked, which implies that until modifications are unblocked, that should hold true.\n\nHowever, isn\u0027t there a possibility that after you drop the lock and load the file, another thread within the process that is loading the file invokes `mmap()`? In that case, you\u0027ll have a mapping while the file is being loaded?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 618,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 630,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "eefd7d2d5c62456fb418c1512efe4ad436dc765f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5e886608_fdf5b38d",
        "filename": "drivers/android/wrapfd.c",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 630,
      "author": {
        "id": 1157738
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-03-13T16:12:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, you are right. It\u0027s not so bad for the load() because it\u0027s not a destructive operation but it\u0027s a bigger problem for the rewrap() or empty() operation. If mmap happens while those are in progress then we will end up mapping memory that is yanked from under us. I\u0027ll add a check in wrap_mmap() to check for ctx-\u003emod_blocked under ctx-\u003elock.",
      "parentUuid": "13224511_87567053",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 618,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 630,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "eefd7d2d5c62456fb418c1512efe4ad436dc765f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0b88e74d_50ac0f38",
        "filename": "drivers/android/wrapfd.c",
        "patchSetId": 16
      },
      "lineNbr": 630,
      "author": {
        "id": 1157738
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-03-13T16:22:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Actually loading and mapping in parallel might be a legit usecase. It\u0027s doable with normal files for example. I think I\u0027ll need two states: mod_blocked and mmap_blocked. Destructive operations (rewrap and empty) should set both of them and load should block only mod_blocked. Them mmap() would check only mmap_blocked and if it\u0027s set then treat it as if that the buffer is gone.",
      "parentUuid": "5e886608_fdf5b38d",
